{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\nWhen a compaction sweep holds the store write lock, a blocked session cannot tell what is\nholding it or whether interrupting it is safe. Both facts are already known to the code and\nneither reaches the operator.\n\nLive incident 2026-08-13 (`vigilant-erasable-pony`): a sweep held\n`.tickets-tracker/.ticket-write.lock` for 24+ minutes at 0.0% CPU. The blocked session saw:\n\n    flock: could not acquire lock after 181s (holder: host=... pid=... held=47s)\n\nA bare pid. Identifying it as compaction required `lsof`/`ps`. The blast radius is host-wide\nacross worktrees, so \"check your own checkout\" does not help.\n\nThe answer is already documented: `compact_trigger.run_sweep` guarantees \"a skipped sweep is a\nno-op for correctness (the events stay live and the next trigger folds them)\". An operator\nholding that guarantee can safely SIGTERM the holder. Looking at an unattributed pid, the safe\nresponse is to wait — which is exactly wrong here. One sweep was killed manually (pid 98440,\n26 minutes) to unblock three agents.\n\n## Approach\nSay what the holder is, and that it is interruptible — at the two places a blocked writer\nalready looks.\n\n**1. Label the lock holder, ambiently.** `lock_owner._owner_stamp` writes the v2 ownership line\n(`rebar-lock v2 host=... ns=... pid=... start=...`) that `describe_lock_holder` renders into\nevery `LockTimeout`. That stamp is written by `_acquire_mkdir` (`lock.py`), reached only through\n`lock.acquire`. Add an optional operation label so the timeout reads\n`... pid=98440 held=181s op=compact-sweep` instead of a bare pid.\n\nThe label must reach `_owner_stamp` WITHOUT editing the acquisition call site: the sweep's\nstore-write-lock acquisition is `lock.acquire()` in `compact_txn._compact_locked`\n(`compact_txn.py:358`), invoked per ticket, and `compact_txn.py` is deliberately OUT of scope\n(it carries the just-landed sweep-perf change `mesodermic-vaporish-seaslug` and must not be\nre-touched). The satisfying mechanism is an AMBIENT label, not a new `acquire` parameter:\n\n- `lock_owner.py` gains a process/thread-local label — a `contextvars.ContextVar` plus an\n  `operation_label(label)` context manager that sets it on enter and resets on exit.\n  `_owner_stamp()` reads that contextvar and, when set, appends a single colon-free `op=<label>`\n  field. Unset (every ordinary writer) ⇒ the stamp is byte-identical to today's.\n- The sweep sets that contextvar around its whole body at its two entry points\n  (`compact_trigger.run_sweep`, `compact.py`'s sweep loop). Every `lock.acquire` beneath that\n  `with` block — including the per-ticket `lock.acquire()` inside `_compact_locked`, which this\n  ticket does NOT modify — writes its stamp with `op=compact-sweep`. Data flow: sweep-entry →\n  contextvar → `_owner_stamp`; the label reaches the stamp ambiently and `compact_txn.py` needs\n  no change. `contextvars` is the right carrier because the label is read synchronously in the\n  same process/thread that later calls `acquire`: the sweep folds inline (`_sweep_one_ticket` →\n  `compact_cli` → `_compact_locked`), so the ambient value is in scope at the acquisition.\n\nThe v2 format's forward-compat contract is preserved exactly: the line is COLON-FREE so an older\nrebar splitting on `host, sep, pid = stamp.partition(\":\")` finds no separator and declines to\nact (bug castoff-tigerseye-ammonite). A new `key=value` field keeps that (the label value is\nsanitized to strip any `:`, `=`, or whitespace, staying one colon-free token), and\n`_parse_v2_stamp` already ignores unknown fields. The label is descriptive only —\n`_stamp_is_stale` / `stamped_file_is_stale` never read `op=`, so reclamation is unaffected.\n\n**2. State the remedy in the error.** `describe_lock_holder` renders the `op=` field into the\nholder string; `LockTimeout` recognises the known interruptible label (`compact-sweep`) in that\nstring and appends a remedy line — that the holder is a compaction sweep, interrupting it loses\nno data, and the next trigger re-folds. An ordinary (unlabelled) holder gets no remedy line.\n\nDeliberately NOT here: auto-killing a long sweep. That is a separate pre-emption policy; the\nreported incidents were all resolved by a human given the right information. Inform first.\n\n## Scope\n- `src/rebar/_store/lock_owner.py` — an ambient operation-label contextvar + `operation_label`\n  context manager; `_owner_stamp` appends an optional colon-free `op=` field (sanitized to one\n  token) when set; a helper mapping a known interruptible label to its remedy text. Never\n  consulted by staleness logic.\n- `src/rebar/_store/lock.py` — render the `op=` label in `describe_lock_holder`; extend the\n  `LockTimeout` message with the safe-to-interrupt remedy when the holder string names a known\n  interruptible operation.\n- `src/rebar/_commands/compact_trigger.py` — wrap `run_sweep`'s body in\n  `lock_owner.operation_label(\"compact-sweep\")` (the ambient carrier — not an edit to any\n  acquisition call), so the store-lock acquisitions it drives via `_compact_locked` stamp it.\n- `src/rebar/_commands/compact.py` — wrap the sweep loop (`compact_all_cli`/`_run_sweep`) in the\n  same `operation_label(\"compact-sweep\")`, so a direct `rebar compact-all` is also labelled.\n- `tests/unit/test_lock_holder_labelling.py` — new tests.\n\nPortability: the mechanism is a colon-free `key=value` field in the lock stamp file plus an\nin-process `contextvars` label — no CI provider, external service, or platform facility — so it\nworks identically anywhere rebar runs, with or without CI.\n\n## Testing\n- A labelled holder appears in `describe_lock_holder` and in the `LockTimeout` message\n  (asserting `op=compact-sweep`, not only a pid).\n- The remedy text is PRESENT for a labelled optional holder, ABSENT for an ordinary writer.\n- An UNLABELLED holder renders byte-identically to today's `describe_lock_holder` output.\n- The stamp stays colon-free with the label; a label containing `:`/`=`/whitespace is sanitized.\n- `_parse_v2_stamp` accepts a stamp carrying an unknown extra field (forward compat).\n- Reclamation is label-blind: a labelled and unlabelled stale lock adjudicate identically.\n- End to end: with `operation_label(\"compact-sweep\")` active, a real `lock.acquire` writes an\n  owner stamp carrying the label — the ambient contextvar reaches `_owner_stamp` through the\n  actual acquisition path `_compact_locked` uses.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A blocked writer's `LockTimeout` names the holder as a compaction sweep — a test asserts\n      the message contains the operation label rather than only a pid.\n- [ ] The remedy text (safe, lossless) is present for a labelled optional holder — a test\n      asserts it.\n- [ ] The remedy text is absent for an ordinary writer — a test asserts it.\n- [ ] An unlabelled holder's rendering is byte-identical to today's — a test pins the format.\n- [ ] The stamp remains colon-free with a label present — a test asserts it.\n- [ ] `_parse_v2_stamp` ignores an unknown extra field — a test asserts it.\n- [ ] Staleness/reclamation ignore the label — a test asserts a labelled and unlabelled stale\n      lock adjudicate identically.\n- [ ] The label reaches the stamp through the real acquisition path via the ambient contextvar —\n      a test drives `lock.acquire` under `operation_label(...)` and asserts the written stamp\n      carries `op=compact-sweep`.\n- [ ] The full local gate set passes.\n- [ ] CI's pytest tiers pass."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786676712959050001,"uuid":"8854d6b0-9b13-4239-97ed-3e11e2b139a2"}